{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_784174029#0_1503342610","relevance":3,"reason":"This article is dedicated to the importance of cultural diversity in nursing and directly explains that nursing ethics require high-quality care for all patients regardless of race, gender, age, disability, or socioeconomic status, and that nurses need cultural diversity awareness to provide that care.","learned":"Cultural diversity/EDI impacts nursing by supporting ethical, equitable, high-quality care for diverse patients and communities."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1436","reason":"This query produced a directly relevant cultural diversity in nursing article; continue for more passages or additional documents explaining impacts on patient care, cultural competence, outcomes, and workforce."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1436"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_784174029#2_1503347350","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1436","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_784174029#2_1503347350\" , \"end_char\": 3417.000000 , \"headings\": \"Understanding the True Importance of Cultural Diversity in Nursing Understanding the True Importance of Cultural Diversity in Nursing What Is Diversity Awareness? Aspects of <cover>Cultural Diversity in Nursing Culturally Competent Nurses Importance of a Culturally Diverse Nursing Workforce</cover>Learn More Recommended Readings: Sources \" , \"segment\": \"Aspects of Cultural Diversity in Nursing Culture has a significant influence in health-related beliefs and practices, according to an article by the American Association of Critical-Care Nurses (AACN). Factors such as language, geography, socioeconomics, gender, and education can play a large role in determining the decisions patients make about medical care, both for themselves and their families. The ANA has identified primary areas of cultural diversity: Health insurance and health care access Racial and ethnic minority communities Mental health Elderly Bariatric/obesity Patients in each of these areas can experience barriers to receiving an appropriate level of care if providers do not account for cultural differences. Nurses who are trained to deliver culturally competent care are prepared "}}
